#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c800bf1-db0e-5a4f-b1f7-79de46c24ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294266} {:answer/id #uuid "7bdc1a32-3ddc-5b62-b443-798708784e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294267} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbfce8f-1539-5097-be10-dbae597cac5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294268} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee8162a-22a0-5c9e-b9db-f9b656661d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294269} {:answer/id #uuid "976eda39-25fa-5c0c-ada1-63cab5aa0334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbfce8f-1539-5097-be10-dbae597cac5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294268} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a0acda-284b-5a31-8855-d2a8a1ff7df1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4878f13e-2679-5b9e-8cae-9094f9bb2d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-141281" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8353b5dc-7cd7-5dd4-8896-24138ab0ad3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247201} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ce6c33-30ba-5cb1-83a3-61b4b3b415ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247202} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8c732c-3a67-5773-95c5-05e65b555e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247203} {:answer/id #uuid "8f17eeee-6e1a-53a8-937d-9232a4cc2c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247204} {:answer/id #uuid "6cce4235-8958-5494-8a97-872bec019cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cce4235-8958-5494-8a97-872bec019cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247205} :answer-field/id #uuid "10cf0431-cc21-5deb-8a40-4019c358ec69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "835d558d-3fba-579b-91a8-f50eb4c92637" :question/math-academy-id "q-15664" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=3$ and $g(1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8af1a7f0-21ea-599e-8ae4-8c70b697fdc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294582} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd70fd7-4169-593d-b17d-6524c8aa73d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294583} {:answer/id #uuid "99bea051-55e3-5f06-9ebe-be38839b0a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294584} {:answer/id #uuid "97e80f02-5fc0-5dbe-8691-00ee2ab26405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294585} {:answer/id #uuid "cc59692d-8e56-544e-8518-ed211520a029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97e80f02-5fc0-5dbe-8691-00ee2ab26405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294585} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfa03b0-59e8-5335-aa32-e5c6bb8083d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0d1d1a5-d9cb-5570-acc7-da72657b9a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163548" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ece31cc5-4493-571f-be4f-43a27342195e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ece31cc5-4493-571f-be4f-43a27342195e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294661} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb1e79f-7789-5ee9-8707-8979eaf696ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c18ce72-f6b8-5ce2-bf10-d7edc42cee9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c18ce72-f6b8-5ce2-bf10-d7edc42cee9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294663} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c91fc5c-380e-5e71-92bf-b258b18e1dfa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "386b24bd-3eff-5335-834a-d78cada3240c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336300" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(3.6\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (2.8\\times {10}^{6})}{3.6\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 4.5\\cdot 2.8}{3.6}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =23.345\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{23.345\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{23.345}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.8316…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.8\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e8a51f-03c5-59c0-b027-d554459af78c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294669} {:answer/id #uuid "712138d7-e620-5be5-b342-8a3843ff1257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294670} {:answer/id #uuid "88282ac6-c4c6-50a3-a829-eed1fc5efb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294671} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f73c9d-7bbc-5af3-8bdc-37f401b89654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294672} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6daef7-cda8-562b-b48f-730cd7e06337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a6daef7-cda8-562b-b48f-730cd7e06337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294673} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bca0655-c64d-5fdc-9b72-2e0dc772b824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "494759ac-e110-5f70-93f7-3b6af7555034" :question/math-academy-id "q-337570" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14035554/assets/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f10507d-8411-501b-bf2a-099f1599108a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294349} {:answer/id #uuid "6b40c74f-5636-54fa-8e48-1cc8dc48453b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294350} {:answer/id #uuid "a8890cb1-796d-5e73-9f8e-477954728915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294351} {:answer/id #uuid "f886e3e2-bc69-5e9f-8cd6-9f9f6b4e1c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294352} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9f71ba-6aae-578e-ae86-a1f2d811e951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b40c74f-5636-54fa-8e48-1cc8dc48453b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294350} :answer-field/id #uuid "0028443c-1e65-58fd-b318-c517c386e7d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bb03c0c-2bf3-5dda-8102-58698d5cc17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c63c6329-411c-5ee5-a6fb-c199983b9670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294823} {:answer/id #uuid "84fccc02-cfba-59ba-965b-cf1df9b5fdd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294824} {:answer/id #uuid "517f550c-47fc-55d5-aaff-7f19a5e4f9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294825} {:answer/id #uuid "69f32dbb-5860-546d-951c-b71903cf942b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294826} {:answer/id #uuid "772c5e5c-a173-5bff-9c9f-6280a74d301e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f32dbb-5860-546d-951c-b71903cf942b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294826} :answer-field/id #uuid "482c157c-a744-5559-9cdf-215aac443a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a38e97ba-c156-5496-ad06-15f48100971c" :question/math-academy-id "q-89885" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(-1,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-1,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =1+(x+y) \\\\  & =x+y+1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y+1)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(-1,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1979b91d-2ac0-5d35-b4cc-1c9c9505b503" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Tanh Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08412b52-4cfb-5412-8537-58ef35295219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294844} {:answer/id #uuid "1be660e5-4491-5cc2-a883-603f1c398d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294845} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0eb8f8-92ae-5019-9d5c-faa2978588e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{1-{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294846} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9c5272-40a5-58cd-912a-33a4cd05f646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294847} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f14128-a178-5bb3-afd9-439f540096b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08412b52-4cfb-5412-8537-58ef35295219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294844} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d1a91e0-b7d1-5713-8e41-61b2622139c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bc13545-d6a0-5612-9a84-04aec6ad0b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95505" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{e}^{x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{({e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot {({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
